# Doubled-word fixtures

WHY THE `.md.fixture` EXTENSION, NOT `.md`. These files carry doubled words
on purpose. `checks/markdown/markdown-scan.nix` scans every tracked `.md` in the
tree — `packages/kiro-cli/checks/fixtures/kiro-steering/alpha.md` and six of its neighbours
are in that corpus today, measured — so a `.md` fixture here would fail the
very check it exists to test. Prettier would also reflow it and destroy the
exact indentation these fixtures encode. Both problems disappear if the
files simply are not `.md`: `find -name '*.md'` does not match them, and no
treefmt formatter claims `.fixture`. The alternative was three new
exclusion entries — the scan's `find`, treefmt's globs, and whatever comes
next — that a future reader has to keep in step; this way there is nothing
to keep in step.

HOW TO ADD ONE. Open with a FIXTURE header comment stating what the fixture
proves, then one `expect:` line per hit, `expect: <line> "<bigram>"`. Line
numbers are 1-based and count the header, so re-check them after editing
anything above the hit. Every fixture that expects NO hit from some
construct must also carry a control doubling in plain prose that IS
expected — otherwise the fixture passes just as happily when the scanner
never reads the file.

Do not quote the literal HTML-comment terminator inside a fixture header.
The parser will stop there and lose expectations that come after it.

Run these with `checks/markdown/doubled-words-fixtures.nix`, which feeds this
directory to `doubled_words_fixtures.py` in the shared scanner store
directory. By hand, from a checkout:

    python3 checks/markdown/doubled-words-fixtures.py checks/markdown/fixtures/doubled-words
